"""Independently reusable steps from the ten finished proof reports."""
FACETS=[
 {'problem':'P0209','key':'rare-state-standardization','namespace':'probability.markov-chains',
  'name':'rational-four-state-rare-mode-standardization','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'For s>=2, let A=1-s^(-2), epsilon=(1-A^2)/(5-4A), B=(5-4A)/(2-A), and R=s^4. For independent stationary Y with rates 0->1=epsilon, 1->0=1-epsilon and fair sign Z flipping at R/2, g=AZ+B(Y-epsilon) has stationary mean zero, variance one, and sup norm two. The product generator has eigenvalues 0,-1,-R,-(R+1), and its additive-functional asymptotic variance rate is 2A^2/R+2B^2 epsilon(1-epsilon).',
  'proof':'Since 1-epsilon=(2-A)^2/(5-4A), B(1-epsilon)=2-A and B^2 epsilon(1-epsilon)=1-A^2. Independence gives the mean and variance. Positivity and epsilon<1/2 give sup norm A+B(1-epsilon)=2. The eigenbasis is 1,Y-epsilon,Z,(Y-epsilon)Z with the displayed eigenvalues. The two orthogonal nonconstant components have covariance A^2 exp(-Ru)+B^2 epsilon(1-epsilon)exp(-u). Twice its integral over positive u gives the asymptotic variance rate, either by exact covariance integration or domination by these integrable exponentials for this fixed chain.'},
 {'problem':'P0459','key':'bilateral-mean','namespace':'probability.random-graphs',
  'name':'bilateral-preference-exact-mean-and-beta-deficit','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'For LK(n,k) obtained by retaining edges among the k cheapest iid continuous costs at both endpoints of K_n, n>=2 and 1<=k<=n-1, the common mean degree mu satisfies 0<=k-mu<=sqrt(k(n-1-k)/(2n)). When k<n-1, mu=(n-1) integral_0^1 P(Bin(n-2,u)<=k-1)^2 du; when k=n-1, mu=k.',
  'proof':'Uniformize the common continuous cost distribution. Given an edge cost u, its two endpoint competitor arrays are disjoint and independent, each accepting it with probability F(u)=P(Bin(n-2,u)<=k-1). Integrate F^2. For k<n-1 use independent kth order statistics U,V from n-2 uniforms, of law Beta(k,n-1-k), to write mu=(n-1)E min(U,V). Therefore k-mu=(n-1)E|U-V|/2, bounded by (n-1)sqrt(2 Var U)/2. The beta variance is k(n-1-k)/[(n-1)^2 n]. The full-degree boundary is deterministic.'},
 {'problem':'C0130','key':'cookie-interval-state','namespace':'probability.random-walks',
  'name':'finite-interval-bounded-cookie-state-representation','kind':'definition',
  'statement':'For a deterministic nearest-neighbor cookie walk stopped on first reaching 0 or L, with at most M cookies at each site and fair transitions after exhaustion, its interior state is (x,c_1,...,c_{L-1}). Here 1<=x<L and c_i in {0,...,M} counts departures from i capped at M. An interior transition consumes the next cookie at x, increments c_x unless exhausted, and moves to x+1 or x-1 with that cookie probability. The resulting finite representation has at most (L-1)(M+1)^(L-1) interior states. This definition is for a fixed interval; it does not make the whole half-line finite-state.',
  'proof':None},
 {'problem':'P0088','key':'adaptive-marked-coupling','namespace':'probability.random-graphs',
  'name':'adaptive-countable-exposure-coupling-failure-bound','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'Consider two adaptive explorations with countable exposure and history spaces. At step j<=m, assume their conditional exposure kernels, whenever past histories agree, have total variation at most epsilon_j uniformly in that history. There is a sequential coupling under which some history mismatch by step m has probability at most sum_{j=1}^m epsilon_j. If the exploration may exceed m steps with probability at most rho, add rho to this bound.',
  'proof':'On each matched history use the discrete maximal coupling: put their common probability mass on the diagonal and couple the residual masses. Its mismatch probability equals the kernel total variation. The probability that the first mismatch occurs at step j is at most epsilon_j times the probability of previous agreement, hence at most epsilon_j. Sum those disjoint first-failure probabilities. Union with the event that more than m steps are needed. Countability ensures these history-indexed couplings form a measurable kernel.'},
 {'problem':'P0336','key':'rectangle-vertical-stability','namespace':'probability.optimal-transport',
  'name':'separated-rectangle-cost-excess-controls-vertical-displacement','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'For any coupling of uniform laws on [0,1]^2 and [2,3]x[0,1], put d=y_1-x_1 and h=y_2-x_2. If its mean Euclidean cost is 2+delta, then delta>=0 and E h^2<=(sqrt(10)+3)delta. Cost 2 is optimal and occurs exactly for couplings with h=0 almost surely; it does not specify the horizontal coupling.',
  'proof':'The marginals force E d=2 and 1<=d<=3, |h|<=1. Rationalization gives sqrt(d^2+h^2)-d=h^2/[sqrt(d^2+h^2)+d], with denominator at most sqrt(10)+3 and at least 2. Integrate the lower bound. Any vertically matched coupling attains cost 2; conversely nonnegative excess has expectation zero only when h=0 almost surely.'},
 {'problem':'C0022','key':'moment-nonsingularity','namespace':'probability.measure',
  'name':'third-moment-difference-with-equivalent-finite-laws','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'The probability laws P=(1/4,1/4,1/4,1/4) and Q=(5/24,9/24,3/24,7/24) on {0,1,2,3} are mutually absolutely continuous. Their first moments equal 3/2 and second moments equal 7/2, but their third moments are respectively 9 and 37/4. Their total variation distance is 1/6. Thus a third-moment difference even after matching the first two moments does not imply singularity.',
  'proof':'All four atoms have strictly positive mass for both laws. Direct sums of x^r against each mass give the displayed moments. Half the sum of absolute mass differences is (1+3+3+1)/48=1/6. The full-support property implies that a zero-probability event for either law is empty on their common support, so the two laws are equivalent.'},
 {'problem':'C0076','key':'one-arm-exponent','namespace':'probability.percolation',
  'name':'one-arm-order-forces-nontrivial-excursion-normalization-exponent','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'Let A_n be events such that c/n<=P(A_n)<=C/n for fixed constants 0<c<=C<infinity. If n^gamma P(A_n) converges to a finite strictly positive number, then gamma=1. The order bounds alone neither prove convergence of n P(A_n) nor identify its constant.',
  'proof':'For gamma<1 the upper bound C n^(gamma-1) tends to zero. For gamma>1 the lower bound c n^(gamma-1) tends to infinity. Both contradict the specified finite positive limit. For gamma=1 the two bounds only confine the mass between c and C and permit oscillation.'},
 {'problem':'C0117','key':'complete-clock-variance','namespace':'probability.random-walks',
  'name':'complete-graph-cover-variance-depends-on-clock','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'For simple random walk on K_n, n>=2 and m=n-1, the cover time has expectation m H_m and maximal expected hitting time m. Its variance is m^2 H_m^(2)-m H_m in discrete time, and m^2 H_m^(2) in continuous time with iid mean-one exponential holding times. Thus the two clock conventions have equal mean cover and hitting times but different cover-time variance.',
  'proof':'With k unvisited vertices, each next transition discovers one with probability k/m from any visited current vertex. The independent discovery gaps are geometric(k/m) by the strong Markov property. Their means sum to m H_m and variances to m^2 H_m^(2)-m H_m. Adding independent mean-one exponential holding times preserves the mean and adds E tau_cov=m H_m to the variance by conditional variance; equivalently the continuous discovery gaps are independent Exp(k/m). A single target has mean hitting time m in either clock.'},
 {'problem':'P0009','key':'bijective-pinball-routing','namespace':'probability.random-walks',
  'name':'manhattan-pinball-bijective-routing-bounded-orbits-periodic','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'In the Manhattan grid, represent a ray by its vertex and incoming horizontal or vertical axis. If each vertex routes the two incoming edges bijectively to its two outgoing edges, either straight or switched by a mirror, the global directed-state map is a bijection. Its forward trajectory has bounded spatial range if and only if it is periodic from its initial state.',
  'proof':'For each incoming directed edge its predecessor vertex is uniquely determined by the street orientation, and the local routing has a unique inverse. Therefore the global state map is invertible. A bounded spatial range contains only finitely many directed states, so two forward states coincide. Apply inverse iterations to deduce a return to the initial state and periodicity from time zero. A periodic trajectory plainly has bounded range.'},
 {'problem':'P0035','key':'tree-product-coordinate','namespace':'probability.random-walks',
  'name':'tree-product-biased-walk-embedded-coordinate-return-probability','kind':'lemma',
  'statement':'For a lambda-biased walk on a finite Cartesian product of (b_i+1)-regular rooted trees, giving closer neighbors weight lambda>0 and farther neighbors weight one, observe coordinate i at its update times. Its radius moves from a positive state up with probability b_i/(b_i+lambda) and down with probability lambda/(b_i+lambda), and moves from zero to one. If lambda<b_i, its probability of returning to zero after leaving zero is lambda/b_i<1. Each coordinate has a uniformly positive update probability. A coordinate satisfying lambda<b_i has only finitely many visits to zero in full time almost surely; all coordinates do so when lambda<min_i b_i.',
  'proof':'Conditioning on selection of coordinate i divides its b_i farther-edge weight and lambda closer-edge weight by b_i+lambda. The full degree is finite and every edge has weight between min(1,lambda) and max(1,lambda), giving a uniform positive lower bound for coordinate selection. Hence coordinate updates occur infinitely often. For the embedded birth-death chain, (lambda/b_i)^r is harmonic away from zero; the finite gambler-ruin formula followed by an upper-boundary limit gives return probability lambda/b_i from radius one. Strong Markov gives a geometric number of returns; every wait at zero is finite by the update bound.'}
]
